{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778977434216,"sessionID":"ses_1ccaede7cffe5Jxx9jj6XWQS7c","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_HgSqe4XY2lyC2bsiB4SaxQNE","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/CLAUDE.md","offset":45,"limit":80},"output":"<path>/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/CLAUDE.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n45: ## GitHub Contribution Process\n46: - most requests from users should follow one of two patterns:\n47:     - you are not confident how to proceed, in which case end with asking a clarifying question (via `gh`)\n48:     - you are confident how to proceed, you make changes, commit on a branch, and open a PR for the user to review\n49: - Check existing terms before adding new ones\n50: - For new terms: provide name, definition, place in hierarchy, and references\n51: - Include PMIDs for all assertions\n52: - Follow naming conventions from parent terms\n53: - always commit in a branch, e.g. issue-NNN\n54: - if there is an existing PR which you started then checkout that branch and continue, rather than starting a new PR (unless you explicitly want to abandon the original PR, e.g. it was on completely the wrong tracks)\n55: - always make clear detailed commit messages, saying what you did and why\n56: - always sign your commits `GitHub Copilot`\n57: - create PRs using `gh pr create ...`\n58: - File PRs with clear descriptions, and sign your PR\n59: \n60: ## Handling GitHub issues and requests\n61: - Use `gh` to read and write issues/PRs\n62: - Sign all commits and PRs as `GitHub Copilot`\n63: \n64: ## TROUBLESHOOTING\n65: \n66: - if your obo file has syntax errors, you can use `robot convert -vvv` to see full trace\n67: - use `robot reason` to validate\n68: \n69: ## Obsoleting terms\n70: \n71: obsolete terms should have no logical axioms (e.g. SubClassOf, EquivalentClasses) on them. Obsolete terms may be replaced by a single\n72: term (so-called obsoletion with exact replacement), or by zero to many `consider` tags.\n73: \n74: Synonyms and xrefs can be migrated judiciously,\n75: \n76: We never do complete merges now, so there should be no `alt_ids` or\n77: disappearing stanzas. If a user asks for a merge, they usually mean\n78: obsoletion with direct replacement.\n79: \n80: No relationship should point to an obsolete term - when you obsolete a term, you may need to also rewire\n81: terms to \"skip\" the obsoleted term.\n82: \n83: ## Other metadata\n84: \n85: - Link back to the issue you are dealing with using the `term_tracker_item`\n86: - All terms should have definitions, with at least one definition xref, ideally a PMID\n87: - All new terms MUST have a timestamp using Dublin Core terms date, e.g.\n88:   `AnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4072102 \"2025-04-29T13:06:36Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)` (where terms: is a prefix for http://purl.org/dc/terms/)\n89: - You can sign terms as `dc:creator \"GitHub Copilot\"` only when creating new terms. You should not add yourself as a creator if you are editing existing terms.\n90: - If one or more ORCID are provided these MUST be added as Dublin Core Terms contributor axioms, e.g. `AnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_0000118 <https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2825-0621>)`\n91: \n92: ```\n93: # Class: obo:CL_4072102 (Purkinje layer interneuron)\n94: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35803588\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4072102 \"A type of GABAergic interneuron residing in the Purkinje cell layer of the cerebellar cortex.\")\n95: AnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4072102 \"2025-04-29T13:06:36Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\n96: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35803588\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType obo:OMO_0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_4072102 \"PLI\")\n97: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4072102 \"Purkinje layer interneuron\")\n98: EquivalentClasses(obo:CL_4072102 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000099 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002100 obo:UBERON_0002979)))\n99: SubClassOf(obo:CL_4072102 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002215 obo:GO_0061534))\n100: ```\n101: \n102: \n103: No relationship should point to an obsolete term - when you obsolete a term, you may need to also rewire\n104: terms to \"skip\" the obsoleted term.\n105: \n106: ## Other metadata\n107: \n108: - Link back to the issue you are dealing with using the `term_tracker_item`\n109: - All terms should have definitions, with at least one definition xref, ideally a PMID\n110: - You can sign terms as `terms:creator \"GitHub Copilot\"` only when creating new terms. You should not add yourself as a creator if you are editing existing terms. (Note: terms: is the prefix for http://purl.org/dc/terms/)\n111: \n112: \n113: ## Relationships\n114: \n115: All terms should have at least one \"is_a\" (SubClassOf to a named class) -- (this can be implicit by a logical definition, see below).\n116: Many terms in this ontology have part_of relationships to UBERON.\n117: \n118: ## Logical definitions\n119: \n120: These should follow genus-differentia form, and the text definition should mirror the logical definition. Example:\n121: \n122: ```\n123: # Class: obo:CL_4072102 (Purkinje layer interneuron)\n124: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35803588\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4072102 \"A type of GABAergic interneuron residing in the Purkinje cell layer of the cerebellar cortex.\")\n\n(Showing lines 45-124 of 133. Use offset=125 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"## GitHub Contribution Process\n- most requests from users should follow one of two patterns:\n    - you are not confident how to proceed, in which case end with asking a clarifying question (via `gh`)\n    - you are confident how to proceed, you make changes, commit on a branch, and open a PR for the user to review\n- Check existing terms before adding new ones\n- For new terms: provide name, definition, place in hierarchy, and references\n- Include PMIDs for all assertions\n- Follow naming conventions from parent terms\n- always commit in a branch, e.g. issue-NNN\n- if there is an existing PR which you started then checkout that branch and continue, rather than starting a new PR (unless you explicitly want to abandon the original PR, e.g. it was on completely the wrong tracks)\n- always make clear detailed commit messages, saying what you did and why\n- always sign your commits `GitHub Copilot`\n- create PRs using `gh pr create ...`\n- File PRs with clear descriptions, and sign your PR\n\n## Handling GitHub issues and requests\n- Use `gh` to read and write issues/PRs\n- Sign all commits and PRs as `GitHub Copilot`\n\n## TROUBLESHOOTING","truncated":true,"loaded":[]},"title":"CLAUDE.md","time":{"start":1778977434182,"end":1778977434214}},"id":"prt_e33516a41001kwnedKVF4pA6sD","sessionID":"ses_1ccaede7cffe5Jxx9jj6XWQS7c","messageID":"msg_e33515518001BIlA5vM4hGgxhR"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778977434225,"sessionID":"ses_1ccaede7cffe5Jxx9jj6XWQS7c","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_rrQvldUS4tLIOjF8fLDu0ykk","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/CLAUDE.md","offset":45,"limit":90},"output":"<path>/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/CLAUDE.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n45: - You should cite publications appropriately, e.g. `def: \"....\" [PMID:nnnn, doi:mmmm]\n46: \n47: ## GitHub Contribution Process\n48: - most requests from users should follow one of two patterns:\n49:     - you are not confident how to proceed, in which case end with asking a clarifying question (via `gh`)\n50:     - you are confident how to proceed, you make changes, commit on a branch, and open a PR for the user to review\n51: - Check existing terms before adding new ones\n52: - For new terms: provide name, definition, place in hierarchy, and references\n53: - Include PMIDs for all assertions\n54: - Follow naming conventions from parent terms\n55: - always commit in a branch, e.g. issue-NNN\n56: - if there is an existing PR which you started then checkout that branch and continue, rather than starting a new PR (unless you explicitly want to abandon the original PR, e.g. it was on completely the wrong tracks)\n57: - always make clear detailed commit messages, saying what you did and why\n58: - always sign your commits `GitHub Copilot`\n59: - create PRs using `gh pr create ...`\n60: - File PRs with clear descriptions, and sign your PR\n61: \n62: ## Handling GitHub issues and requests\n63: - Use `gh` to read and write issues/PRs\n64: - Sign all commits and PRs as `GitHub Copilot`\n65: \n66: ## TROUBLESHOOTING\n67: \n68: - if your obo file has syntax errors, you can use `robot convert -vvv` to see full trace\n69: - use `robot reason` to validate\n70: \n71: ## Obsoleting terms\n72: \n73: obsolete terms should have no logical axioms (e.g. SubClassOf, EquivalentClasses) on them. Obsolete terms may be replaced by a single\n74: term (so-called obsoletion with exact replacement), or by zero to many `consider` tags.\n75: \n76: \n77: Synonyms and xrefs can be migrated judiciously,\n78: \n79: We never do complete merges now, so there should be no `alt_ids` or\n80: disappearing stanzas. If a user asks for a merge, they usually mean\n81: obsoletion with direct replacement, as here:\n82: \n83: Example:\n84: \n85: ```\n86: # Class: obo:CL_4072102 (Purkinje layer interneuron)\n87: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35803588\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4072102 \"A type of GABAergic interneuron residing in the Purkinje cell layer of the cerebellar cortex.\")\n88: AnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4072102 \"2025-04-29T13:06:36Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\n89: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35803588\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType obo:OMO_0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_4072102 \"PLI\")\n90: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4072102 \"Purkinje layer interneuron\")\n91: EquivalentClasses(obo:CL_4072102 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000099 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002100 obo:UBERON_0002979)))\n92: SubClassOf(obo:CL_4072102 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002215 obo:GO_0061534))\n93: ```\n94: \n95: No relationship should point to an obsolete term - when you obsolete a term, you may need to also rewire\n96: terms to \"skip\" the obsoleted term.\n97: \n98: ## Other metadata\n99: \n100: - Link back to the issue you are dealing with using the `term_tracker_item`\n101: - All terms should have definitions, with at least one definition xref, ideally a PMID\n102: - You can sign terms as `dc:creator \"GitHub Copilot\"` only when creating new terms. You should not add yourself as a creator if you are editing existing terms.\n103: \n104: ## Relationships\n105: \n106: All terms should have at least one \"is_a\" (SubClassOf to a named class) -- (this can be implicit by a logical definition, see below).\n107: Many terms in this ontology have part_of relationships to UBERON.\n108: \n109: ## Logical definitions\n110: \n111: These should follow genus-differentia form, and the text definition should mirror the logical definition. Example:\n112: \n113: ```\n114: # Class: obo:CL_4072102 (Purkinje layer interneuron)\n115: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35803588\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4072102 \"A type of GABAergic interneuron residing in the Purkinje cell layer of the cerebellar cortex.\")\n116: AnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4072102 \"2025-04-29T13:06:36Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\n117: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35803588\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType obo:OMO_0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_4072102 \"PLI\")\n118: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4072102 \"Purkinje layer interneuron\")\n119: EquivalentClasses(obo:CL_4072102 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000099 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002100 obo:UBERON_0002979)))\n120: SubClassOf(obo:CL_4072102 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002215 obo:GO_0061534))\n121: ```\n122: \n123: The reasoner can find the most specific `is_a`, so it's OK to leave this off.\n124: \n\n(End of file - total 124 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- You should cite publications appropriately, e.g. `def: \"....\" [PMID:nnnn, doi:mmmm]\n\n## GitHub Contribution Process\n- most requests from users should follow one of two patterns:\n    - you are not confident how to proceed, in which case end with asking a clarifying question (via `gh`)\n    - you are confident how to proceed, you make changes, commit on a branch, and open a PR for the user to review\n- Check existing terms before adding new ones\n- For new terms: provide name, definition, place in hierarchy, and references\n- Include PMIDs for all assertions\n- Follow naming conventions from parent terms\n- always commit in a branch, e.g. issue-NNN\n- if there is an existing PR which you started then checkout that branch and continue, rather than starting a new PR (unless you explicitly want to abandon the original PR, e.g. it was on completely the wrong tracks)\n- always make clear detailed commit messages, saying what you did and why\n- always sign your commits `GitHub Copilot`\n- create PRs using `gh pr create ...`\n- File PRs with clear descriptions, and sign your PR\n\n## Handling GitHub issues and requests\n- Use `gh` to read and write issues/PRs\n- Sign all commits and PRs as `GitHub Copilot`","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"__pr_result__/CLAUDE.md","time":{"start":1778977434201,"end":1778977434222}},"id":"prt_e33516a53001pzuoa1u0U1lL8E","sessionID":"ses_1ccaede7cffe5Jxx9jj6XWQS7c","messageID":"msg_e33515518001BIlA5vM4hGgxhR"}}
